import Game.Levels.Projection.L09_GramSchmidtBoss

World "Projection"
Title "Projection"

Introduction "
Hefferon Three.VI. Projecting a vector into a line splits it into a part along the line and a
part orthogonal to it. Repeating this is the Gram–Schmidt process, which turns any basis into an
orthogonal one. Orthogonal bases are worth having because coordinates in them need no linear
system: the $i$-th coordinate of $\\vec v$ is just $\\vec v\\cdot\\vec\\kappa_i/\\vec\\kappa_i\\cdot\\vec\\kappa_i$.
And, as Sheet 8 shows, Gram–Schmidt can go badly wrong in floating point.
"
